From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Matt Mower <mdmower@cmphys.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 00:21:45 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] [bugs #3357] Keep FUNC_HASBODY for a function that is
 declared again

addSymChain() replaces the symbol of an earlier declaration at file
scope with the symbol of the new declaration. Where the earlier symbol
was the symbol of a definition, the new symbol did not have
FUNC_HASBODY, and the functions that test the flag treated the function
as one without a body:

    int func (int c) { return c; }
    int func (int);

- emitRegularMap() did not add func to the publics unless the
  translation unit used func after the declaration. For the ports that
  define the label of a function with one colon, such as mcs51 and
  stm8, the symbol was then local, and other modules could not call the
  function.
- checkFunction() accepted a second definition of func, and the
  assembler reported the label that is defined twice.
- createInterruptVect() reported error 74 (function 'main' undefined)
  for a main() that is declared again after its definition.

Functions with a declaration before their definition were not affected
by the first item, because checkFunction() adds the symbol of such a
definition to the publics.

addSymChain() now sets FUNC_HASBODY for the new symbol when code was
generated for the earlier one. Inline definitions for which code is not
generated do not get the flag, because emitRegularMap() would declare
their functions global in a module that does not define them.

Functions that are declared before and after their definition now get
two .globl lines, one for the symbol that checkFunction() adds to the
publics and one for the symbol that emitRegularMap() adds.

Adds tests/bug-3357.c, whose function is in fwk/lib/extern1.c, and
valdiag test bug-3357.c.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/SDCCsymt.c                       |  7 ++++
 support/regression/fwk/lib/extern1.c | 11 ++++++
 support/regression/tests/bug-3357.c  | 16 +++++++++
 support/valdiag/tests/bug-3357.c     | 52 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 4 files changed, 86 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 support/regression/tests/bug-3357.c
 create mode 100644 support/valdiag/tests/bug-3357.c

diff --git a/src/SDCCsymt.c b/src/SDCCsymt.c
index 88836c354..53f5e8e27 100644
--- a/src/SDCCsymt.c
+++ b/src/SDCCsymt.c
@@ -1753,6 +1753,13 @@ addSymChain (symbol **symHead)
           if (csym->ival && !sym->ival)
             sym->ival = csym->ival;
           sym->generated |= csym->generated;
+          /* sym replaces csym in the symbol table. emitRegularMap () makes functions
+             public and checkFunction () rejects a second body where FUNC_HASBODY is set,
+             so sym needs the flag when csym is a definition. Inline definitions for which
+             code was not generated are excluded, because the module would declare the
+             function global and not define it. */
+          if (csym->generated && IFFUNC_HASBODY (csym->type))
+            FUNC_HASBODY (sym->type) = true;
 
           if (IS_EXTERN (sym->etype) && IFFUNC_ISINLINE (csym->type))
             {
diff --git a/support/regression/fwk/lib/extern1.c b/support/regression/fwk/lib/extern1.c
index c3974972e..01701c90c 100644
--- a/support/regression/fwk/lib/extern1.c
+++ b/support/regression/fwk/lib/extern1.c
@@ -26,3 +26,14 @@ has_undefined_symbol2(void) {
   /* empty */
 }
 
+/* needed by tests/bug-3357.c
+   the declaration has to follow the definition,
+   and this file must not call the function */
+int
+bug_3357 (int c)
+{
+  return c + 1;
+}
+
+int bug_3357 (int c);
+
diff --git a/support/regression/tests/bug-3357.c b/support/regression/tests/bug-3357.c
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..ffbd044c7
--- /dev/null
+++ b/support/regression/tests/bug-3357.c
@@ -0,0 +1,16 @@
+/* bug-3357.c
+   Functions that were declared again after their definition, and not called
+   in their translation unit, did not get a global symbol for some ports, so
+   other translation units could not call them. bug_3357 is defined in
+   fwk/lib/extern1.c, and a declaration follows the definition there.
+ */
+
+#include <testfwk.h>
+
+int bug_3357 (int c);
+
+void
+testBug (void)
+{
+  ASSERT (bug_3357 (41) == 42);
+}
diff --git a/support/valdiag/tests/bug-3357.c b/support/valdiag/tests/bug-3357.c
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..bbedca04e
--- /dev/null
+++ b/support/valdiag/tests/bug-3357.c
@@ -0,0 +1,52 @@
+/* bug-3357.c
+
+   After a declaration that followed the definition of a function, the symbol
+   of the function did not record the body, and the compiler accepted a second
+   definition.
+ */
+
+#ifdef TEST1
+int f (int c)
+{
+  return c;
+}
+
+int f (int c);
+
+int f (int c) /* ERROR */
+{
+  return c + 1;
+}
+#endif
+
+#ifdef TEST2
+/* Declarations before and after the one definition are valid. */
+int g (int c);
+
+int g (int c)
+{
+  return c;
+}
+
+int g (int c);
+extern int g (int c);
+#endif
+
+#ifdef TEST3
+static int h (int c)
+{
+  return c;
+}
+
+static int h (int c);
+
+static int h (int c) /* ERROR */
+{
+  return c + 1;
+}
+
+int i (void)
+{
+  return h (1);
+}
+#endif
